    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No worries, I just like to think of entanglement in terms of goblins, it's a fun way to wrap my head around it. But, of course, the actual physics behind entanglement is much more fascinating.\n\nEntanglement is indeed related to the wave function, specifically the concept of superposition. When two particles interact, their wave functions become entangled, meaning that the state of one particle is dependent on the state of the other. This is often visualized as a \"spooky action at a distance\" where the particles are connected by an invisible thread.\n\nThink of it like two separate, identical, and independent little boxes (goblin dens, perhaps?) containing a coin. Each box has a 50% chance of having heads or tails. When the boxes are separate, the coins are independent, and the probability of getting heads or tails in one box doesn't affect the other. But when the boxes are entangled, the coins become connected, and the state of one box instantly affects the other, regardless of the distance between them.\n\nThe mathematical framework that describes this phenomenon is the Schrödinger equation, which is a partial differential equation that governs the time-evolution of the wave function. The",
      "source": "target",
